BUG 3 – Frontend Stuck on Loading After API Error

1. What was the issue?
In useTasks.js, loading was set to true before the API request.
When the API request failed, the catch block only set the error:
setError(err.message);
But setLoading(false) was never called.
Because of this, loading remained true even after the API failed.

2. How did I find it?
I checked the error-handling logic in useTasks.js and then checked
TaskTable.jsx.

TaskTable checks the loading state before the error state:

if (loading) {
    return <div>Loading tasks...</div>;
}

if (error) {
    return <div>Error: {error}</div>;
}

Therefore, when the API failed:

loading = true
error = "Request failed..."

The Loading message continued to display and the actual error
message was never shown.

3. What did I change?
I added setError(null) before starting the request and used
finally() to always set loading to false.

The API call now follows:

.then()  -> update tasks
.catch() -> set error
.finally() -> set loading to false

4. Why did I change it?

The finally() block executes whether the API request succeeds or
fails. This ensures that the loading state is always reset.

This allows the user to see the actual error message instead of
being stuck on "Loading tasks..." forever.

Result:

The frontend now correctly stops loading and displays an error
message when the API request fails.